q('A dengue patient’s fever resolves on day five, but severe abdominal pain, repeated vomiting and lethargy develop. What is the best interpretation?', 'Warning signs near the critical phase requiring urgent reassessment rather than reassurance from defervescence',[
 ('The falling temperature proves uncomplicated recovery','Deterioration often begins around defervescence.'),('The symptoms require only an antipyretic dose increase','They signal possible severe progression.'),('Dengue can be excluded because fever is no longer present','The critical phase can occur as fever subsides.')],
 'Temperature improvement can coincide with circulatory risk', '**Plasma leakage may become important around defervescence.**',['Assess perfusion, urine output and hematocrit trends.','Persistent vomiting and abdominal pain are warning signs.'], '**Determine admission and monitoring needs promptly.**',['Assess bleeding and organ dysfunction as well.','Do not use the platelet count as the sole severity measure.'],[['Febrile phase','Fever and constitutional symptoms'],['Critical phase','Can begin near defervescence'],['Warning signs','Require prompt clinical reassessment']],[DG,DP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with dengue has cool extremities, tachycardia, delayed capillary refill and BP 110/95 mmHg. What is most concerning?', 'Compensated shock with a narrowed pulse pressure despite a preserved systolic pressure',[
 ('Normal circulation because systolic pressure exceeds 100','Pulse pressure and perfusion show danger.'),('Hypertension requiring immediate vasodilator treatment','The pattern is compatible with circulatory compromise.'),('Absence of shock until systolic pressure reaches 60','Waiting for profound hypotension can be dangerous.')],
 'Systolic pressure can remain apparently normal during early dengue shock', '**A narrow pulse pressure is a key warning sign.**',['Peripheral perfusion and mental status add information.','Hypotension can be a later finding.'], '**Treat and monitor through the dengue shock pathway.**',['Use titrated fluids and frequent reassessment.','Follow hematocrit and assess bleeding to guide the next step.'],[['Pulse pressure','Systolic minus diastolic'],['Compensated shock','Narrow pulse pressure and poor perfusion'],['Late deterioration','Overt hypotension']],[DP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A dengue patient has worsening perfusion and a rising hematocrit during the critical phase. Which mechanism is most likely?', 'Plasma leakage causing intravascular volume loss and hemoconcentration',[
 ('Dilution from excessive fluid as the usual cause of rising hematocrit','Dilution tends to lower hematocrit.'),('Severe acute blood loss as the only possible explanation for the rising value','Bleeding commonly lowers the value after equilibration or resuscitation.'),('A platelet count artifact as the direct cause of hemoconcentration','The changes involve different measurements.')],
 'Hematocrit is useful only when interpreted with perfusion and treatment', '**Loss of plasma relative to red cells raises hematocrit.**',['Baseline values and administered fluid affect the trend.','A high value in an unstable patient supports ongoing leakage.'], '**Titrate isotonic crystalloid according to the clinical pathway.**',['Reassess after each rate change or bolus.','Monitor for both inadequate resuscitation and overload.'],[['Rising hematocrit','Hemoconcentration'],['Unstable circulation','Intravascular depletion concern'],['Action','Titrated fluid and close review']],[DG,DP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A dengue patient remains hemodynamically unstable while hematocrit falls, with evidence of significant gastrointestinal bleeding. What is the best next principle?', 'Assess and treat major bleeding, including appropriate blood transfusion rather than repeated crystalloid alone',[
 ('Assume every falling hematocrit proves safe recovery','Instability and bleeding change the meaning.'),('Treat only with prophylactic platelets regardless of red-cell loss','The patient needs bleeding-directed resuscitation.'),('Continue unlimited crystalloid without reassessing hemorrhage','This can delay necessary blood replacement and cause overload.')],
 'A falling hematocrit can mean dilution, reabsorption or hemorrhage', '**The accompanying clinical state determines interpretation.**',['Persistent shock with falling hematocrit raises concern for bleeding.','Assess the amount and consequences of blood loss.'], '**Use the severe-dengue bleeding pathway.**',['Provide appropriate blood products and supportive care.','Reassess perfusion and avoid unnecessary fluid accumulation.'],[['Falling value + stable','Could reflect dilution/recovery'],['Falling value + shock/bleeding','Consider hemorrhage'],['Management','Bleeding-directed resuscitation']],[DP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A stable dengue patient has thrombocytopenia but no significant bleeding or procedure-related indication. Which strategy is generally appropriate?', 'Avoid routine prophylactic platelet transfusion solely to correct the count',[
 ('Transfuse until every platelet count is normal','This does not improve outcomes routinely and adds risk.'),('Use platelet transfusion as a substitute for circulatory assessment','It does not treat plasma leakage.'),('Ignore every new bleeding symptom because prophylaxis is not routine','Clinical bleeding needs separate urgent assessment.')],
 'A low platelet count is not the whole bleeding mechanism', '**Routine prophylactic transfusion has no established sufficient benefit in this setting.**',['Volume overload and transfusion reactions are relevant harms.','Severe bleeding and selected procedures require individualized management.'], '**Monitor the patient rather than treating the number alone.**',['Assess mucosal bleeding, hemodynamics and hematocrit.','Avoid drugs that worsen platelet function.'],[['Count alone','Not an automatic transfusion trigger'],['Clinical bleeding','Separate management decision'],['Monitoring','Perfusion, hematocrit and bleeding']],[WA,DP])
q('Which fluid-management approach is appropriate for dengue shock?', 'Carefully titrated isotonic crystalloid with frequent reassessment of perfusion, hematocrit and fluid tolerance',[
 ('A fixed large fluid volume continued regardless of response','Dengue leakage and reabsorption phases change rapidly.'),('Diuretics as the first treatment for untreated intravascular shock','They can worsen depletion in that phase.'),('No IV fluid under any circumstance because edema can occur','Shock requires prompt appropriately titrated resuscitation.')],
 'Dengue requires a balance between depletion and overload', '**The fluid rate is adjusted to the patient’s response.**',['Urine output and peripheral perfusion are important.','Hematocrit helps distinguish leakage and bleeding in context.'], '**Reduce fluids when the resuscitation need resolves.**',['Recovery can return extravascular fluid to the circulation.','Cardiac, renal and respiratory findings influence tolerance.'],[['During shock','Restore effective circulating volume'],['After response','Reduce to the required rate'],['Recovery','Avoid reabsorption-phase overload']],[WA,DG])
q('On day three of a compatible dengue illness, which laboratory combination is appropriate under CDC guidance?', 'NAAT plus IgM, or NS1 antigen plus IgM',[
 ('IgG alone as definitive proof of current dengue','Past infection and cross-reactivity limit that interpretation.'),('A negative early IgM alone to exclude dengue','Antibody may not yet be detectable.'),('A late-only test strategy that delays clinical care for weeks','Management must proceed according to the current clinical syndrome.')],
 'Diagnostic yield changes with illness day', '**RNA or NS1 is useful during the first seven days.**',['IgM becomes more useful as the illness progresses.','A negative acute test may require a convalescent sample.'], '**Do not delay supportive care while awaiting confirmation.**',['Other flaviviruses can complicate serological interpretation.','Record onset date and relevant exposure history.'],[['First week','NAAT or NS1 with IgM'],['After day seven','IgM is the primary test'],['Negative acute result','Does not always exclude infection']],[DT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which analgesic approach is preferred for uncomplicated dengue symptoms when otherwise safe?', 'Paracetamol with avoidance of aspirin and NSAIDs during the bleeding-risk phase',[
 ('Aspirin as the preferred antipyretic because platelets are low','It can worsen platelet dysfunction and bleeding risk.'),('Ibuprofen as mandatory first-line therapy in all cases','NSAIDs can increase bleeding and renal risks.'),('Routine systemic steroids to shorten every fever','Routine steroids are not recommended for uncomplicated dengue.')],
 'Symptom treatment must account for hemostatic and organ vulnerability', '**Avoid medicines that increase bleeding risk.**',['Use appropriate paracetamol dosing and account for liver disease.','Hydration and warning-sign monitoring remain central.'], '**Escalate care for abdominal pain or persistent vomiting rather than simply adding analgesics.**',['Pain can be a warning sign.','Assess comorbidities and concomitant antithrombotic therapy individually.'],[['Preferred symptom drug','Paracetamol when suitable'],['Avoid','Aspirin/NSAIDs in the risk context'],['Warning pain','Reassess rather than mask']],[WA,DG])
q('Which feature can classify dengue as severe even without major external bleeding?', 'Severe organ involvement or plasma leakage causing shock/respiratory distress',[
 ('Only a positive NS1 test with normal physiology','It establishes infection evidence, not severity.'),('Any mild isolated platelet reduction','That does not by itself establish severe dengue.'),('Only persistence of fever beyond one day','Duration alone is insufficient.')],
 'Severe dengue is broader than hemorrhage', '**Shock, severe bleeding and severe organ involvement are major categories.**',['Hepatic, neurological and cardiac disease can be important.','The absence of visible bleeding does not ensure safety.'], '**Use clinical and organ-function assessment.**',['Escalate monitoring and support for severe manifestations.','Evaluate alternative or concurrent causes of organ dysfunction.'],[['Leakage','Shock or respiratory compromise'],['Bleeding','Clinically severe hemorrhage'],['Organ involvement','Severe hepatic, CNS, cardiac or other dysfunction']],[DG,WA])
q('During dengue recovery, a patient’s perfusion improves and extravascular fluid begins to return to the circulation. What should happen to IV fluids?', 'Reassess and reduce or stop them as clinically appropriate to prevent overload',[
 ('Continue the peak shock rate until platelets reach the reference range','Platelet recovery is not the fluid-rate endpoint.'),('Increase fluid automatically whenever hematocrit falls despite stable circulation','Falling hematocrit can accompany dilution/reabsorption.'),('Ignore new dyspnea because the fever has resolved','It can signal fluid overload or another complication.')],
 'The same infusion can be life-saving in shock and harmful during recovery', '**Fluid reabsorption increases intravascular volume.**',['Ongoing intake must be matched to current needs and losses.','Cardiorenal vulnerability increases overload risk.'], '**Follow clinical status and balance rather than one laboratory target.**',['Assess oxygenation, lung findings, urine output and hemodynamics.','Use decongestion only when indicated and safe for the current phase.'],[['Critical phase','Leakage can deplete volume'],['Recovery','Reabsorption restores volume'],['Action','Adjust or stop unnecessary IV fluid']],[DP,DG],style='clinical_scenario')

